- step 2: Fit simplified LTE model of CO emission
  - method: Parametric nebula with Keplerian disk plus radial hourglass outflow, power-law density and temperature, compared to maps [3.1 Simple modeling: "The density and temperature ($n$ and $T$) are assumed to depend solely on the distance to the center and the equator with potential laws."]
  - parameter: V(2e15 cm)=2.5 km/s; inclination ~45 deg (±15); X(13CO)~2e-5; X(12CO)~1e-4 [3.1 Simple modeling: "We adopt a value of $\sim$ 45$^\circ$ ($\pm$ 15$^\circ$)."]
  - assumption: LTE level populations; distance 1 kpc [3.1 Simple modeling: "We assume LTE populations for the involved rotational levels."]
  - validation: Comparison of synthetic and observed channel maps and p-v diagrams [A.3 Comparison: "The model reproduces most of the observed features, but some problems are still present."]
- result: Central stellar mass = 1 ± +0.6/-0.4 Msun [3.1 Simple modeling: "The uncertainty of the deduced central mass is 1$^{+0.6}_{-0.4}$ $M_{\mbox{\ensuremath{\odot}}}$."]
- result: Total nebular mass = 4e-3 ± not stated Msun [3.1 Simple modeling: "The total mass derived from our fitting is 4 10$^{-3}$ $M_{\mbox{\ensuremath{\odot}}}$"]
- limitation (authors): ~25% flux filtered out, model overpredicts faint extended emission [2 Observations: "a small fraction of $\sim$ 25% of the flux has been filtered out in the maps of $^{12}$CO $J$=3$-$2."]
- limitation (authors): Inclination poorly determined [3.1 Simple modeling: "The axis inclination with respect to the plane of the sky is poorly determined"]
- not stated (card): Model code and its source are not named or released; Line-free channels and continuum subtraction method; Image size, cell size, and final rms; Rest frequencies and velocity reference; Fitting procedure and goodness-of-fit metric (apparently by eye); Calculation of kinematical age and disk lifetime; Disk width and shape details

### 2016ApJ...823L..18H: Evidence for a CO Desorption Front in the Outer AS 209 Disk (2016; protoplanetary_disks, astrochemistry; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Does CO isotopologue emission in the AS 209 disk show CO enhancement (desorption) in the outer disk, beyond the expected CO snowline?
- step 1: Calibrate and image data
  - method: Modified NAASC calibration script in CASA, phase self-cal, uv continuum subtraction, CLEAN with Keplerian masks [Observations and Data Reduction: "After subtracting the continuum in the uv-plane, each line was imaged and CLEANed with Keplerian rotation masks."]
  - software: CASA 4.4.0
  - parameter: 0.1 km/s channels [Observations and Data Reduction: "Channel rms for 0.1 km s$^{-1}$ bins and integrated flux values are listed in Table [1](#Table1)."]
  - validation: Continuum flux consistent with SMA measurement [Observations and Data Reduction: "which is consistent with Submillimeter Array observations of AS 209 presented in (cite: 2011ApJ...734...98O)"]
- step 3: Toy model of CO desorption ring and synthetic observations
  - method: Parametric density/temperature model with Gaussian ring in CO surface density; RADMC-3D line images; sampled at observed uv with VIS_SAMPLE; noise added; CLEANed in CASA; compared with and without ring [A toy model for CO desorption in the ou…: "After computing sky brightness images at the orientation of the AS 209 disk, model visibilities were produced at the same spatial frequencies as the AS 209 data by using Python package VIS_SAMPLE."]
  - software: RADMC-3D, VIS_SAMPLE, CASA 4.4.0
  - parameter: Sigma_c=5e-5 g/cm2, gamma=1, r_c=100 AU, B=3, r_ring=150 AU, sigma_ring=20 AU [A toy model for CO desorption in the ou…: "we set $\Sigma_c = 5\times 10^{-5} \text{ g cm}^{-2}$, $\gamma = 1$, $r_c = 100$ AU, $B = 3$, $r_\text{ring} = 150$ AU, and $\sigma_\text{ring} = 20$ AU."]
  - parameter: turbulent broadening xi = 0.01 km/s [A toy model for CO desorption in the ou…: "the turbulent broadening parameter was set to $\xi =$ 0.01 km s$^{-1}$"]
  - assumption: LTE; Keplerian velocities; ISM isotopologue ratios 69 and 557; T_atm,10=150 K, q_atm=0.5; C18O traces CO column [A toy model for CO desorption in the ou…: "Local thermal equilibrium was assumed because CO and its isotopologues have low critical densities relative to disk gas densities"]
  - validation: Qualitative morphology comparison with and without ring (B=0) [A toy model for CO desorption in the ou…: "only the model with an outer CO ring, shown in Figure [3](#fig3), reproduces the central peak and ring in the C$^{18}$O emission, and the shoulder in $^{13}$CO emission."]
- result: 1.4 mm continuum flux density = 252 ± 25 mJy [Observations and Data Reduction: "is 252 $\pm$ 25 mJy"]
- result: 12CO J=2-1 integrated flux = 7.5 ± 0.8 Jy km/s [Table 1: "7.5$\pm$0.8"]
- result: 13CO J=2-1 integrated flux = 2.1 ± 0.2 Jy km/s [Table 1: "2.1$\pm$0.2"]
- result: C18O J=2-1 integrated flux = 0.54 ± 0.05 Jy km/s [Table 1: "0.54$\pm$0.05"]
- limitation (authors): Thermal vs non-thermal desorption contributions not constrained; narrow optically thick dust rings need higher resolution [Discussion: "Further observations and modeling are necessary to constrain the relative contributions of thermal versus non-thermal processes."]
- not stated (card): tclean/clean parameters: weighting/robust, cell size, image size, deconvolution threshold; Which SPWs were used for self-calibration and solution interval; Velocity range and channel width of final image cubes beyond 0.1 km/s; Details of the Keplerian mask parameters; Stellar mass/distance adopted in toy model beyond 0.9 Msun and 120 pc; grid setup and photon/radiative transfer settings in RADMC-…

